// CloudTrail Logging Tampered (T1685.002)
//
// One signal, expressed structurally: a CloudTrail configuration change that REDUCES what
// is recorded. Stopping or deleting a trail, turning off multi-Region coverage or global
// service events, disabling log file integrity validation, repointing the destination
// bucket, or rewriting the event selectors so that management events, write events, one
// service's control plane, or one principal's activity stop matching.
//
// This query does what the Sigma rules structurally cannot. `PutEventSelectors` carries
// the RESULTING selector configuration rather than the delta — AWS states the cross-type
// case ("If you apply `AdvancedEventSelectors` to a trail, any existing `EventSelectors`
// are overwritten") and demonstrates the same-type case, there being no patch verb on the
// API. So the sharpest test on it is an ABSENCE test across an array: an advanced
// selector set that contains no `eventCategory Equals Management` condition means
// management events are no longer logged at all. No substring match expresses "no element
// of this array satisfies P". The parse below does.
//
// It does NOT cover the two things that are not CloudTrail events at all — a trail that is
// still logging but can no longer deliver (an S3-side break, visible only on
// GetTrailStatus), and the account that stops producing CloudTrail rows entirely. Both are
// in the commented companions at the bottom. Deploy those too; on this technique the
// absence detector is the one that fires when the primary query has nothing left to see.
//
// Dialect: Sentinel / Azure Log Analytics KQL. NOT CloudWatch Logs Insights.
AWSCloudTrail
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where EventSource == "cloudtrail.amazonaws.com"
// The event-data-store calls are here for one reason, and it is a scoping fact that is
// easy to get backwards. On a TRAIL, AWS documents `userIdentity.arn` as a selector field
// for DATA events and network activity events only — the management-event field set for a
// trail is `eventCategory`, `eventSource`, `readOnly`. Filtering MANAGEMENT events by
// caller identity is available only on an EVENT DATA STORE, so the sharpest form of
// self-exclusion does not arrive on PutEventSelectors at all. It arrives here.
| where EventName in ("StopLogging", "DeleteTrail", "UpdateTrail",
                      "PutEventSelectors", "PutInsightSelectors",
                      "CreateEventDataStore", "UpdateEventDataStore")
// Denied attempts are a different incident — see the companion at the bottom. Counting a
// failed StopLogging as a stopped trail sends a responder to restore a trail that never
// went down, and hides the actor who was refused.
| where isempty(ErrorCode)
| extend Req = parse_json(RequestParameters)
//
// FIELD SHAPE. The API Reference names these parameters `Name`, `S3BucketName`,
// `IsMultiRegionTrail`, `EventSelectors[].ReadWriteType`,
// `AdvancedEventSelectors[].FieldSelectors[].Field`. CloudTrail writes the
// INITIAL-LOWERCASE form into requestParameters, and AWS publishes the proof twice — a
// success-path `StartLogging` record reading `"requestParameters": {"name": "myTrail"}`,
// and an `UpdateTrail` record reading
// `"requestParameters": {"name": "myTrail2", "isMultiRegionTrail": true}`
// (CloudTrail concepts and log file examples, retrieved 2026-08-29). The UpdateTrail one
// is published as an ERROR example and carries `errorCode: TrailNotFoundException`, so
// cite it for the casing and not for what a successful call does. The PascalCase half of every
// `coalesce` below is therefore belt-and-braces for a re-shaping ingestion pipeline, not a
// hedge against not knowing — an unmatched key here returns empty and the rule would
// report a clean account, which is the one failure mode worth paying two extra terms for.
// The flattened top-level columns used below — UserIdentityArn, UserIdentityAccessKeyId,
// EventName, EventSource, ErrorCode, SourceIpAddress, AwsRegion — are Sentinel columns,
// not dotted JSON paths; only RequestParameters needs parse_json.
| extend TrailName = coalesce(tostring(Req.name), tostring(Req.Name),
                              tostring(Req.trailName), tostring(Req.TrailName),
                              tostring(Req.eventDataStore), tostring(Req.EventDataStore))
| extend NewBucket = coalesce(tostring(Req.s3BucketName), tostring(Req.S3BucketName))
//
// UpdateTrail behaves as a PARTIAL update: an omitted optional parameter leaves the
// setting unchanged. AWS demonstrates this rather than stating it — its
// `update-trail --name my-trail --is-multi-region-trail` example passes one flag and the
// returned trail still carries S3BucketName, IncludeGlobalServiceEvents,
// LogFileValidationEnabled and IsOrganizationTrail unchanged. So a `false` here was
// explicitly sent, and the widening (`true`) is a different string. This is the whole
// difference between a narrowing and a routine trail edit, and it is why matching
// `EventName == "UpdateTrail"` alone cannot work.
| extend MultiRegionOff = tostring(coalesce(Req.isMultiRegionTrail, Req.IsMultiRegionTrail)) =~ "false"
| extend GlobalEventsOff = tostring(coalesce(Req.includeGlobalServiceEvents, Req.IncludeGlobalServiceEvents)) =~ "false"
| extend ValidationOff = tostring(coalesce(Req.enableLogFileValidation, Req.EnableLogFileValidation)) =~ "false"
//
// SHAPE GUARDS. Both selector collections are arrays when present and ABSENT otherwise —
// and absent is not the same as empty, which is what the trailing `dynamic([])` in each
// coalesce is for. Without it the value is null, `array_length(null)` is null, the padding
// test below evaluates neither true nor false, `iff` falls to its else branch and hands
// `mv-apply` a null: every StopLogging, DeleteTrail and UpdateTrail row is then dropped
// before it is ever scored, and the query returns a clean account while a trail is down.
// Normalising absent-to-empty here is what makes the padding deterministic.
// `HasAdvSelectors` is captured BEFORE padding, because the absence test further down is
// only meaningful for a request that actually supplied advanced selectors — a
// basic-selector request has no advanced array and must not read as "management events
// removed".
| extend EvSelRaw  = coalesce(Req.eventSelectors, Req.EventSelectors, dynamic([]))
| extend AdvSelRaw = coalesce(Req.advancedEventSelectors, Req.AdvancedEventSelectors, dynamic([]))
| extend HasAdvSelectors = array_length(AdvSelRaw) > 0
| extend InsSelRaw = coalesce(Req.insightSelectors, Req.InsightSelectors, dynamic([]))
// Insights is turned off by passing an EMPTY list — that is the documented mechanism, so
// the empty array IS the signal and must survive the padding below.
| extend InsightsEmptied = EventName == "PutInsightSelectors" and array_length(InsSelRaw) == 0
//
// PADDING, and this is the trap. `mv-apply` over an EMPTY array DROPS the source row, so
// every StopLogging and UpdateTrail event would silently vanish from the result set before
// it was ever evaluated. Pad each array to one empty object so the aggregation returns
// zero rather than removing the row.
| extend EvSelArr  = iff(array_length(EvSelRaw)  == 0, dynamic([{}]), EvSelRaw)
| extend AdvSelArr = iff(array_length(AdvSelRaw) == 0, dynamic([{}]), AdvSelRaw)
| mv-apply es = EvSelArr on (
    summarize
      BasicMgmtOff  = countif(tostring(coalesce(es.includeManagementEvents, es.IncludeManagementEvents)) =~ "false"),
      BasicReadOnly = countif(tostring(coalesce(es.readWriteType, es.ReadWriteType)) =~ "ReadOnly"),
      BasicExcluded = countif(array_length(coalesce(es.excludeManagementEventSources, es.ExcludeManagementEventSources, dynamic([]))) > 0)
  )
| mv-apply aes = AdvSelArr on (
    // Same absent-vs-empty normalisation as above, and for the same reason: the padded
    // `{}` element carries no fieldSelectors at all, so without the trailing dynamic([])
    // this inner mv-apply is handed a null and drops the row.
    mv-apply fs = iff(array_length(coalesce(aes.fieldSelectors, aes.FieldSelectors, dynamic([]))) == 0,
                      dynamic([{}]),
                      coalesce(aes.fieldSelectors, aes.FieldSelectors, dynamic([]))) on (
      extend FName = tostring(coalesce(fs.field, fs.Field))
      | extend FEq = coalesce(fs.equals, fs.Equals, dynamic([]))
      | summarize
          // set_has_element tests the ARRAY. strcat_array would strip the quotes and an
          // equality test on the joined string drops every multi-element case.
          MgmtCategory = countif(FName =~ "eventCategory" and set_has_element(FEq, "Management")),
          SelfExclude  = countif(FName =~ "userIdentity.arn"),
          AdvReadOnly  = countif(FName =~ "readOnly" and set_has_element(FEq, "true"))
    )
    | summarize MgmtCategory = sum(MgmtCategory), SelfExclude = sum(SelfExclude), AdvReadOnly = sum(AdvReadOnly)
  )
//
// The absence test. Advanced selectors were supplied and NONE of them asks for management
// events, so the trail records no control-plane activity from this point on. Sigma cannot
// state this; it is the reason this file exists alongside the Sigma.
| extend AdvMgmtRemoved = HasAdvSelectors and MgmtCategory == 0
| extend ScopeReduced =
      EventName in ("StopLogging", "DeleteTrail")
   or MultiRegionOff or GlobalEventsOff or ValidationOff
   or isnotempty(NewBucket)
   or BasicMgmtOff > 0 or BasicReadOnly > 0 or BasicExcluded > 0
   or AdvMgmtRemoved or SelfExclude > 0 or AdvReadOnly > 0
   or InsightsEmptied
| where ScopeReduced
//
// `contains`, never `has`. Every operand that matters here — "cloudtrail.amazonaws.com",
// "userIdentity.arn", an ARN, a bucket name with dots — is punctuated, and `has` is
// whole-term matching that breaks on exactly those characters.
| extend Detail = case(
    EventName == "StopLogging",  strcat("logging stopped on ", TrailName),
    EventName == "DeleteTrail",  strcat("trail deleted: ", TrailName),
    // Scope the wording to what the selector can actually do on the resource it was
    // applied to. Calling the trail case "management logging" would send a responder
    // hunting for a control-plane gap that is not there, and understate the EDS case.
    SelfExclude > 0 and EventName in ("CreateEventDataStore", "UpdateEventDataStore"),
        "event data store selector filters MANAGEMENT events by userIdentity.arn — one principal's control-plane activity is no longer recorded",
    SelfExclude > 0,             "trail selector filters DATA/network-activity events by userIdentity.arn — one principal's data access is no longer recorded",
    AdvMgmtRemoved,              "advanced selectors no longer request eventCategory=Management",
    BasicMgmtOff > 0,            "includeManagementEvents set false",
    BasicReadOnly > 0 or AdvReadOnly > 0, "restricted to read-only — every mutating call is now unlogged",
    BasicExcluded > 0,           "a management event source excluded from the trail",
    MultiRegionOff,              "multi-Region coverage turned off — shadow trails deleted",
    GlobalEventsOff,             "global service events (IAM, STS) dropped",
    ValidationOff,               "log file integrity validation disabled — digest chain breaks in one hour",
    isnotempty(NewBucket),       strcat("destination repointed to ", NewBucket),
    InsightsEmptied,             "Insights event collection turned off",
    "scope reduced")
| summarize
    Changes    = count(),
    Trails     = make_set(TrailName, 5),
    Details    = make_set(Detail, 8),
    Events     = make_set(EventName, 5),
    Buckets    = make_set(NewBucket, 5),
    SourceIPs  = make_set(SourceIpAddress, 5),
    AccessKeys = make_set(UserIdentityAccessKeyId, 5),
    AnyStopped   = max(toint(EventName in ("StopLogging", "DeleteTrail"))),
    AnyMgmtExcl  = max(toint(SelfExclude > 0 and EventName in ("CreateEventDataStore", "UpdateEventDataStore"))),
    AnyDataExcl  = max(toint(SelfExclude > 0 and EventName == "PutEventSelectors")),
    AnyMgmtGone  = max(toint(AdvMgmtRemoved or BasicMgmtOff > 0)),
    AnyWriteGone = max(toint(BasicReadOnly > 0 or AdvReadOnly > 0)),
    FirstSeen  = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen   = max(TimeGenerated)
    by UserIdentityArn, AwsRegion, bin(TimeGenerated, 15m)
| extend Verdict = case(
    AnyMgmtExcl == 1,
        "IDENTITY EXCLUDED FROM MANAGEMENT-EVENT LOGGING on an event data store — no benign form of this, P0",
    AnyDataExcl == 1,
        "IDENTITY EXCLUDED FROM DATA-EVENT LOGGING on a trail — management events still recorded, but this principal's data access is not, P0",
    AnyStopped == 1,
        "TRAIL STOPPED OR DELETED — restore logging first, then investigate, P0",
    AnyMgmtGone == 1,
        "MANAGEMENT EVENTS NO LONGER RECORDED while the trail still reports healthy, P0",
    AnyWriteGone == 1,
        "TRAIL RESTRICTED TO READ-ONLY — every mutating call is now invisible, P0",
    "TRAIL SCOPE REDUCED — confirm against the selector baseline before dispositioning, P1")
| project FirstSeen, LastSeen, UserIdentityArn, AwsRegion, Verdict, Details, Trails,
          Buckets, Events, Changes, AccessKeys, SourceIPs
| order by LastSeen desc
//
// ============================================================================
// COMPANION 1 — THE ABSENCE DETECTOR. Deploy this. It is the only rule here that
// fires when the technique has already succeeded.
// ============================================================================
// Everything above needs a CloudTrail event to exist. Once a trail is stopped, no further
// event from that Region reaches this workspace, so the query above goes quiet — and quiet
// is indistinguishable from a calm Tuesday unless something is watching for it.
//
// Threshold basis: NOT an observed count. Set `MinRowsPerRegion` from your own quietest
// hour per Region, measured over at least four weeks including a weekend, and set the bin
// no shorter than your ingestion lag or the rule alerts on the pipeline rather than on the
// account. A Region with no workload legitimately produces zero rows; exclude it by name
// rather than by lowering the threshold for every Region at once.
//
// let MinRowsPerRegion = 1;
// let ActiveRegions = dynamic(["us-east-1", "eu-west-1"]);   // your own list
// let Window = 2h;
// AWSCloudTrail
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(1d)
// | where AwsRegion in (ActiveRegions)
// | summarize Rows = count() by AwsRegion, Slot = bin(TimeGenerated, Window)
// | where Rows < MinRowsPerRegion
// | extend Verdict = "NO CLOUDTRAIL RECORDS FROM THIS REGION IN THE WINDOW — trail stopped,
//                     delivery broken, selectors narrowed, or the shipper is down. All four
//                     look identical here; GetTrailStatus separates them."
// | order by Slot desc
//
// A note on why that verdict is worded as four possibilities rather than one. The source
// alert this replaces groups by the log-stream identifier rather than by Region, which
// makes it fire once for the whole account and name none of the four causes. Grouping by
// Region at least tells the responder where to point GetTrailStatus.
//
// ============================================================================
// COMPANION 2 — denied CloudTrail writes, kept SEPARATE from the success path
// ============================================================================
// A principal collecting AccessDenied across CloudTrail writes is establishing whether it
// can blind the account. That is reconnaissance and must not share a verdict with a trail
// that is actually down.
//
// AWSCloudTrail
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(1d)
// | where EventSource == "cloudtrail.amazonaws.com"
// | where ErrorCode contains "AccessDenied"
// | where EventName in ("StopLogging","DeleteTrail","UpdateTrail","PutEventSelectors",
//                       "PutInsightSelectors","DeleteEventDataStore","UpdateEventDataStore",
//                       "StopEventDataStoreIngestion")
// | summarize Denials = count(), Attempted = make_set(EventName, 8),
//             FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated), LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
//             by UserIdentityArn, AwsRegion, bin(TimeGenerated, 15m)
// | where Denials > 2
// | order by Denials desc
//
// ============================================================================
// WHAT NO QUERY OVER THIS TABLE CAN SEE
// ============================================================================
// 1. DELIVERY BROKEN S3-SIDE. A bucket policy that no longer permits
//    cloudtrail.amazonaws.com to `s3:PutObject`, a deleted bucket, or a KMS key CloudTrail
//    can no longer use, all stop log files arriving while the trail reports
//    `IsLogging: true` and emits no CloudTrail event of its own. The only signal is
//    `GetTrailStatus` -> `LatestDeliveryError` (S3 log files), `LatestDigestDeliveryError`
//    (digest files) and `LatestCloudWatchLogsDeliveryError` (the CloudWatch Logs path),
//    polled per trail per Region. `IsLogging` alone is NOT a health check — treating it as
//    one produces a clean bill of health on a trail delivering nothing.
//    CloudTrail retries delivery for 30 days after a misconfiguration, so fixing the
//    bucket policy inside that window recovers the missed files.
//
// 2. A NARROWING THAT ONLY A DIFF REVEALS. `PutEventSelectors` submits the selector set
//    whole, and a set that drops one data-resource ARN or tightens one StartsWith
//    prefix is a narrowing that looks identical to the widening that added it. Compare
//    `get-event-selectors` output against a stored baseline on a schedule — the same
//    shape as a code-hash drift detector, and the only control that survives a compromised
//    logging pipeline whose ARN is on every allowlist here.
//
// 3. ANOTHER ACCOUNT'S TRAIL. Nothing in this workspace sees a member account degrade its
//    own trail unless that account ships here too. An ORGANIZATION trail is the structural
//    answer: AWS documents that users in member accounts "do not have sufficient
//    permissions to delete organization trails, turn logging on or off, change what types
//    of events are logged, or otherwise change an organization trail in any way".
//
// ============================================================================
// TELEMETRY THAT SURVIVES THE TAMPERING — and one that does not
// ============================================================================
// SURVIVES. CloudTrail **Event history** is a separate 90-day per-Region record of
// MANAGEMENT events, and AWS states that "changes you make to your event data stores or
// trails do not affect the event history". `aws cloudtrail lookup-events` reads it. A
// stopped trail therefore does not blind management-event forensics inside 90 days in the
// Region where the calls landed — it blinds the pipeline that feeds this table, the
// durable archive beyond 90 days, and every event category Event history never held.
//
// SURVIVES. GuardDuty consumes CloudTrail management events "directly from CloudTrail
// through an independent and duplicated stream of events", and AWS states plainly that
// "your CloudTrail configurations don't affect how GuardDuty consumes and processes the
// event logs". `Stealth:IAMUser/CloudTrailLoggingDisabled` fires on a successful trail
// deletion or update; its DEFAULT SEVERITY IS LOW, which is the wrong disposition for this
// event and needs raising in your severity mapping.
//
// DOES NOT SURVIVE. EventBridge is not an independent path. AWS: "To record events with
// one of the CloudTrail `detail-type` values, you must enable a CloudTrail trail with
// logging." An EventBridge rule on `AWS API Call via CloudTrail` is therefore downstream of
// the thing being disabled. Separately, a default `ENABLED` rule matches only WRITE
// management events; read-only management events need the rule state
// `ENABLED_WITH_ALL_CLOUDTRAIL_MANAGEMENT_EVENTS`.
//
// ============================================================================
// ERROR STRINGS
// ============================================================================
// Denials: `AccessDenied` (IAM-evaluated) and `AccessDeniedException` (service-evaluated).
// Match both prefix-tolerantly; confirm which your account produces against a real denied
// event. CloudTrail does NOT use the EC2 `Client.` prefix.
//
// Non-denial errors these calls throw, which must NOT be counted as probing. Enumerated
// from each operation's Errors section, not guessed, and the set is NOT uniform — see the
// PutInsightSelectors exception immediately below the common list:
//   common to StopLogging, DeleteTrail, UpdateTrail and PutEventSelectors — all eleven, on
//   all four:      TrailNotFoundException, InvalidTrailNameException,
//                  CloudTrailARNInvalidException, InvalidHomeRegionException,
//                  OperationNotPermittedException, UnsupportedOperationException,
//                  ConflictException, ThrottlingException,
//                  NotOrganizationMasterAccountException, NoManagementAccountSLRExistsException,
//                  InsufficientDependencyServiceAccessPermissionException
//   PutInsightSelectors carries nine of those eleven: it does NOT list
//                  ConflictException or InsufficientDependencyServiceAccessPermissionException.
//                  A "common set" written as twelve-across-five would be wrong twice.
//   UpdateTrail     InsufficientS3BucketPolicyException, InsufficientSnsTopicPolicyException,
//                   InsufficientEncryptionPolicyException, S3BucketDoesNotExistException,
//                   InvalidS3BucketNameException, InvalidS3PrefixException,
//                   InvalidSnsTopicNameException, InvalidKmsKeyIdException,
//                   KmsKeyNotFoundException, KmsException, KmsKeyDisabledException (deprecated),
//                   InvalidCloudWatchLogsLogGroupArnException, InvalidCloudWatchLogsRoleArnException,
//                   CloudWatchLogsDeliveryUnavailableException, CloudTrailAccessNotEnabledException,
//                   CloudTrailInvalidClientTokenIdException, InvalidParameterException,
//                   InvalidParameterCombinationException, InvalidEventSelectorsException,
//                   OrganizationsNotInUseException, OrganizationNotInAllFeaturesModeException,
//                   TrailNotProvidedException (deprecated)
//   PutEventSelectors    InvalidEventSelectorsException
//   PutInsightSelectors  InvalidInsightSelectorsException, InsufficientS3BucketPolicyException,
//                        InsufficientEncryptionPolicyException, S3BucketDoesNotExistException,
//                        KmsException, InvalidParameterException, InvalidParameterCombinationException
//
// `InvalidHomeRegionException` is the operationally interesting one: DeleteTrail,
// UpdateTrail and PutEventSelectors must be called from the trail's HOME Region, and
// StopLogging must be for any trail enabled in all Regions. None of them works against a
// shadow trail. An actor who guesses the Region wrong leaves a denied-shaped event in the
// Region it guessed, which is a free early warning — and a RESPONDER who guesses wrong
// gets zero rows from every query in this file, which is why the playbook resolves the
// home Region from `describe-trails` before it queries anything.
//
// ============================================================================
// PREVENTION
// ============================================================================
// A second trail that the account under attack cannot reach — an organization trail owned
// by the management account, or a trail in a separate logging account delivering to a
// bucket in a third account with Object Lock — is the only control that holds when the
// principal degrading the trail is legitimately allowed to administer CloudTrail. Every
// allowlist in this file necessarily exempts that principal.
